			<h3>PASSPORT AND VISA</h3>

						<p>There is a visa required to enter India. A foreign citizen needs a signed passport valid for at least six months from the date of arrival in India with two blank pages for stamping by the Immigration Officer and a current visa to enter India. For more latest information on Passport &amp; Visa requirement, please <a href="https://indianvisaonline.gov.in/">CLICK HERE</a> to visit the website of the Government of India. Additionally, travelers need to have visa with specific permission if they are visiting a restricted area - Ladakh, Sikkim, Andaman &amp; Nicobar Islands etc. 
						<br/><br/>
						e-TOURIST VISA: <a href="https://indianvisaonline.gov.in/evisa/tvoa.html" target="_blank">CLICK HERE</a> to read detailed information on requirements and how to apply online. 
						<br/><br/>
						IMPORTANT: <u>In case your travel includes a visit to Nepal or Bhutan and requires you to return to India to take your international flight home or touring in India, you would have to apply for a double or multiple entry Indian visa.</u> In addition, you would also require a visa to enter Nepal &amp; Bhutan.
						
						</p>
						<h5>SIKKIM PERMIT</h5>
						<p><b>Foreign nationals require an Inner Line Permit to enter Sikkim, even for regular sightseeing. 
						There are three methods to obtain this permit:</b></p>
						<ol>
							<li>Visa Application: If you are applying for an Indian visa, you can mention Sikkim, 
							and a 15-day permit may be stamped directly on your visa. Please note that this option 
							is not available to travellers entering India on an e-visa.</li>
							<li>District Magistrate's Office: If you are already in Darjeeling, 
							you can apply for the permit at the District Magistrate's office. Please be aware 
							that permits are not issued on weekends or public holidays.</li>
							<li>Entry Checkposts: You can obtain the permit at the time of entry into Sikkim from checkposts such as the 
							Rangpo or Melli checkposts. Rangpo operates until 8:00 PM, while Melli is open until 7:00 PM. 
							To apply, you will need to present your passport, a copy of your visa, and two passport-sized photographs. 
							It is also advisable to carry photocopies of your passport and visa.</li>
						</ol>
						<h6>Restrictions for Foreign Nationals:</h6>
						<ul>
							<li>Foreigners are not permitted to visit Nathu La Pass, Lachen, Lachung, or Tsomgo Lake without a special
							Protected or Restricted Area Permit.</li>
							<li>Solo travel is prohibited in these areas; a minimum of two foreigners must travel 
							together and be accompanied by a local guide.</li>
						</ul>
						<h6>Additional Considerations:</h6>
						<ul>
							<li>Travellers who are citizens of, or were born in, China, Pakistan, Myanmar, Hong Kong, Macau, Nigeria, or Afghanistan, or who have visited any of these countries within the past three months, 
							must have their permits approved by the Ministry of Home Affairs in Delhi. Their permits cannot be arranged locally.</li>
							<li>For travellers holding a Taiwan passport, permits to visit Sikkim can only be applied for through the online FRRO 
							portal. Applicants must log into the website, upload the necessary documents, and submit their application. 
							Once approved, the permits will be updated online and available for download.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>Indian nationals do not require a permit to visit open areas in Sikkim, such as Gangtok, Pelling, Ravangla, 
						and Namchi. However, permits are necessary for entry into protected and border areas such as Tsomgo Lake, Baba Mandir, 
						North Sikkim (including Lachen, Lachung, Yumthang Valley, and Gurudongmar Lake). Additionally, a special permit 
						is required for Nathu La Pass, which is accessible to tourists every day except Monday. The permit is arranged one 
						day before the intended travel date. However, it is essential to note that permits may be suspended at any time due to 
						adverse weather conditions, road issues, or security concerns. To obtain a permit, you will need to provide two
						passport-sized photographs and a valid form of identification, such as a voter ID, driver's license, or passport. 
						Please note that PAN and Aadhaar cards are not accepted in the state of Sikkim. For children, a school ID can be used 
						as proof of identity.</p>

<br/>
						<h3>CUSTOMS GUIDELINES:</h3>
						<p>Any traveller can bring foreign exchange into India from a place outside India. However, Declaration of foreign exchange/currency has to be made before the custom officers in the following cases:<p/>
						<ul style="list-style-type:none;">
						  <li>(a) Where the value of foreign currency notes exceeds US$ 5000 or equivalent.</li>
						  <li>(b) Where the aggregate value of foreign exchange (in the form of currency notes, bank notes, traveler cheques etc.) exceeds US$ 10,000 or its equivalent.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>Please <a href="https://eoi.gov.in/eoisearch/MyPrint.php?8760?001/0029" target="_blank">CLICK HERE</a> to refer detailed information on Government of India website.</p>
						<br/>
						
						<h3>WEATHER:</h3>
						<p>India has three major seasons: winter, summer and the monsoon. The winter months (November-March) are pleasant 
						throughout India with bright sunny days. In the northern plains, the minimum temperature at times drops steeply and there is snowfall in the hills. In southern and eastern India, however, December and January are pleasantly cool, never really cold.<br><br>It is during the summer months (April-June) that hill resorts such as Shimla, Kashmir valley, Darjeeling and Mount Abu provide cool retreats.<br><br>The south-west monsoon usually breaks about the beginning of June on the west coast and reaches elsewhere later. With the exception of the south-eastern areas, India receives the major share of its rainfall between June and September. The south-eastern areas receive most of the rainfall from the north-east monsoon between mid-October and December-end.<br><br>Traditionally, India has been most popular in the winter months. However, with easy availability of air-conditioned hotels, transport and leisure facilities (such as dining and shopping, the summer months have become equally popular.</p>
						<br/>
						
